,2,0,2,2,2,2,2,0],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,0,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,0,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2]],"output":[[3,2,4],[1,1,8],[7,3,2]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The input is a grid of blocks separated by full lines of colour 0. Each block is mostly one colour, with some 0 \"holes\" scattered through it.\n\nIn pair 1, there are zero rows at y=7 and zero columns at x=6 and x=13. That makes a 2×3 arrangement of blocks:\n- top row: 4, 8, 3\n- bottom row: 1, 2, 8\n\nThe output is exactly that 2×3 grid. Pairs 2 (2×2) and 3 (3×2) work the same way.\n\nSo the rule is: find the all-zero rows and columns, and treat the runs of non-zero lines between them as panel bands. Each panel becomes one output cell, whose colour is the panel's most common non-zero colour.\n\nSome panels contain stray cells of another colour, and the most-common vote handles them. I group consecutive divider lines into runs, so a doubled divider would not create empty panels.\n\nThe test has zero rows at y=4 and y=19, and zero columns at x=7 and x=15. That gives a 3×3 output. The rows should read 3 2 4 / 1 1 8 / 7 3 2.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    zr = [all(v == 0 for v in g[y]) for y in range(g.shape[0])]\n    zc = [all(v == 0 for v in g[:, x]) for x in range(g.shape[1])]\n\n    def runs(flags):\n        out, start = [], None\n        for i, f in enumerate(flags + [True]):\n            if not f and start is None:\n                start = i\n            elif f and start is not None:\n                out.append((start, i - 1)); start = None\n        return out\n\n    rb, cb = runs(zr), runs(zc)\n    out = np.zeros((len(rb), len(cb)), dtype=int)\n    emit(out.copy(), f\"Zero divider lines split the grid into {len(rb)}x{len(cb)} panels; allocate one cell per panel.\")\n    for i, (y0, y1) in enumerate(rb):\n        for j, (x0, x1) in enumerate(cb):\n            p = g[y0:y1 + 1, x0:x1 + 1]\n            vals, cnts = np.unique(p[p != 0], return_counts=True)\n            out[i, j] = vals[np.argmax(cnts)] if len(vals) else 0\n        emit(out.copy(), f\"Panel row {i}: each cell takes its panel's dominant non-zero colour.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"panels":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The input is a grid of blocks separated by full lines of colour 0."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"e rule is: find the all-zero rows and columns, and treat the runs of non-zero lines between them as panel bands."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each panel becomes one output cell, whose colour is the panel's most common non-zero colour."}],"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each panel becomes one output cell, whose colour is the panel's most common non-zero colour."}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each block is mostly one colour, with some 0 \"holes\" scattered through it."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The output is exactly that 2×3 grid."}],"scale":[{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Zero divider lines split the grid into 2x3 panels; allocate one cell per panel."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Zero divider lines split the grid into 2x2 panels; allocate one cell per panel."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Zero divider lines split the grid into 3x2 panels; allocate one cell per panel."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"cdbe00bf91d4bd7838388d9ab09e1ff4dc8e6f71d41f49985123957871553294","task_sha256":"01152e0c0c2448b80dfb295c57c6d6959a1276667443f127464fc060edf72d04","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
